  },
  "questions": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "1 ÷ (1/2 + 1/3) =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "5"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "6/5"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "5/6"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "1/5"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "Do the bracket first: 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. Then 1 ÷ 5/6 = 6/5. Why the others tempt: 5 takes the reciprocal of each fraction separately (2 + 3). 5/6 stops after the bracket. 1/5 inverts the wrong number.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 2,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If A is 25% more than B, then B is what percent less than A?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "25%"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "20%"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "80%"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "125%"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "Let B = 100, so A = 125. B is less than A by 25 out of 125, which is 20%. The percent is now taken of A, a bigger base. Why the others tempt: 25% reuses the old base. 80% is B as a percent of A, not how much less. 125% is A as a percent of B.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 3,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "A shopkeeper marks goods 25% above cost price and then allows a 10% discount. His profit percent is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "12.5%"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "10%"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "15%"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "35%"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Let cost = 100. Marked price = 125. Selling price = 125 × 0.90 = 112.5. Profit = 12.5, so 12.5%. Why the others tempt: 15% subtracts 10 from 25, but the discount applies to the marked price. 35% adds them. 10% ignores the mark-up.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 4,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The ratio of two numbers is 3: 4 and their LCM is 48. The sum of the two numbers is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "7"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "14"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "48"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "28"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "The numbers are 3k and 4k. Since 3 and 4 share no factor, their LCM is 12k. 12k = 48 gives k = 4, so the numbers are 12 and 16. Sum = 28. Why the others tempt: 7 is only 3 + 4. 14 halves the sum. 48 is the LCM itself.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 5,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "12 men working 8 hours a day complete a job in 10 days. How many days will 15 men working 6 hours a day need?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "8 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "12 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "13 1/3 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "10 2/3 days"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "Total work = men × hours × days = 12 × 8 × 10 = 960 man-hours. Now 15 × 6 = 90 man-hours per day, so days = 960 ÷ 90 = 10 2/3. Why the others tempt: 8 days ignores the change in hours. 13 1/3 days ignores the change in the number of men. 12 is a guess.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 6,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "Two trains 100 m and 150 m long run in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 60 km/h. The time they take to cross each other is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "2.5 seconds"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "9 seconds"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "25 seconds"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "45 seconds"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "Opposite directions: add the speeds, 100 km/h = 100 × 5/18 = 250/9 m/s. Distance = sum of lengths = 250 m. Time = 250 ÷ (250/9) = 9 s. Why the others tempt: 45 s uses the difference of speeds (same direction). 2.5 s divides metres by km/h with no conversion. 25 s uses a wrong conversion.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 7,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "Rs. 10,000 is invested at 10% compound interest per annum. The compound interest earned in 2 years is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Rs. 2,000"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Rs. 2,200"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Rs. 2,100"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Rs. 12,100"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "Year 1: 10% of 10,000 = 1,000, so the amount is 11,000. Year 2: 10% of 11,000 = 1,100. Interest = 1,000 + 1,100 = 2,100. (The amount is 12,100.) Why the others tempt: 2,000 is simple interest. 12,100 is the amount, not the interest. 2,200 doubles the second year’s interest.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 8,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "A can do a job in 10 days and B in 15 days. They work together for 3 days, then A leaves. B finishes the rest in:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "6 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "9 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "12 days"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "7.5 days"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "Together: 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6 per day. In 3 days they do 3/6 = 1/2 of the job. The remaining 1/2 at B’s rate 1/15 per day takes 7.5 days. Why the others tempt: 12 subtracts 3 from B’s 15 days. 6 and 9 use the wrong fraction of work left.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 9,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The average age of 20 students is 15 years. When the teacher’s age is included, the average becomes 16. The teacher’s age is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "16 years"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "36 years"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "31 years"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "21 years"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "Total for 21 people = 21 × 16 = 336. Students’ total = 20 × 15 = 300. Teacher = 336 − 300 = 36. Why the others tempt: 16 is just the new average. 31 is 15 + 16. 21 is the number of people.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 10,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "√0.0081 =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "0.9"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "0.009"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "0.0009"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "0.09"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "0.0081 = 81/10,000, so its square root is 9/100 = 0.09. Check: 0.09 × 0.09 = 0.0081. Why the others tempt: A square root halves the number of decimal places (4 becomes 2). 0.9 would be the root of 0.81.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 11,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The smallest number greater than 10 that leaves remainder 3 when divided by 4, 5 and 6 is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "63"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "3"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "60"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "123"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Such numbers are LCM(4, 5, 6) × k + 3. The LCM is 60, so the numbers are 3, 63, 123, … The smallest above 10 is 63. Why the others tempt: 3 leaves remainder 3 but is not greater than 10. 60 leaves remainder 0. 123 works but is not the smallest.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 12,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The best estimate of 4.98 × 19.9 ÷ 0.495 is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "20"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "100"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "200"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "2,000 Algebra (Q13–Q21)"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "Round each number: 5 × 20 ÷ 0.5 = 100 ÷ 0.5 = 200. Dividing by 0.5 doubles the result. Why the others tempt: 100 forgets to divide by 0.5. 20 and 2,000 are decimal-place slips.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 13,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If a + b = 7 and a² + b² = 29, then ab =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "10"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "5"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "20"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "78"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², so 49 = 29 + 2ab. Then 2ab = 20 and ab = 10. Why the others tempt: 20 is 2ab. 5 halves ab again. 78 adds 49 and 29.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 14,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If x − y = 3 and xy = 10, then x³ − y³ =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "117"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "−63"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "27"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "90"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Use x³ − y³ = (x − y)³ + 3xy(x − y) = 27 + 3 × 10 × 3 = 27 + 90 = 117. (Check: x = 5, y = 2 gives 125 − 8 = 117.) Why the others tempt: 27 stops after cubing. 90 is only the second term. −63 subtracts the second term (27 − 90).",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 15,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "Which of the following is a factor of x² − x − 12?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "x + 4"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "x − 3"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "x + 12"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "x − 4"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "Find two numbers with product −12 and sum −1: they are −4 and +3. So x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3). Only x − 4 is offered. Why the others tempt: x + 4 and x − 3 use the right numbers with the wrong signs. x + 12 borrows the constant term.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 16,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "√50 + √18 − √8 =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "6√2"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "5√2"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "7√2"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "√60"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Simplify each surd: √50 = 5√2, √18 = 3√2, √8 = 2√2. Then 5√2 + 3√2 − 2√2 = 6√2. Why the others tempt: √60 adds and subtracts the numbers under the roots. Surds combine only after each is simplified to like surds.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 17,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "1/(x − 1) − 1/(x + 1) =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "2/(x² − 1)"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "0"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "2x/(x² − 1)"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "−2/(x² − 1)"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Common denominator (x − 1)(x + 1) = x² − 1. Numerator: (x + 1) − (x − 1) = 2. Result: 2/(x² − 1). Why the others tempt: 0 assumes the two terms cancel. −2 flips the subtraction. 2x/(x² − 1) invents an x in the numerator.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 18,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "For x ≠ 2, (x − 2)/(2 − x) =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "1"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "0"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "x − 2"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "−1"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "2 − x = −(x − 2), so the fraction is (x − 2)/[−(x − 2)] = −1. Why the others tempt: 1 ignores that the denominator is the negative of the numerator. 0 treats the cancelled factor as zero.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 19,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "(x³y²)² ÷ (x²y) =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "x⁴y⁴"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "x⁵y³"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "x⁴y³"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "x⁶y³"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "(x³y²)² = x⁶y⁴. Divide by x²y: x⁶⁻² × y⁴⁻¹ = x⁴y³. Why the others tempt: x⁶y³ forgets to divide by x². x⁴y⁴ forgets to divide by y. x⁵y³ subtracts only 1 from the exponent of x.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 20,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If x + 1/x = 3, then x² + 1/x² =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "5"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "9"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "11"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "7"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "Square both sides: x² + 2 + 1/x² = 9. So x² + 1/x² = 9 − 2 = 7. Why the others tempt: 9 forgets the middle term 2. 11 adds 2 instead of subtracting. 5 subtracts 4.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 21,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If (x + 2) is a factor of x² + 3x + k, then k =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "−2"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "4"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "6 Equations (Q22–Q28)"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "x + 2 is a factor, so x = −2 makes the expression zero: 4 − 6 + k = 0, so k = 2. Check: x² + 3x + 2 = (x + 1)(x + 2). Why the others tempt: −2 is the root, not k. 4 forgets the 3x term. 6 uses x = +2.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 22,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If 3^(x + 1) = 81, then x =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "4"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "3"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "27"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "81 = 3⁴, so x + 1 = 4 and x = 3. Why the others tempt: 4 forgets the +1. 27 is 81 ÷ 3. 2 slips the other way.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 23,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The sum of three consecutive even numbers is 78. The largest of them is:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "28"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "24"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "26"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "30"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "Let the numbers be x, x + 2, x + 4. Then 3x + 6 = 78 and x = 24. The numbers are 24, 26, 28, so the largest is 28. Why the others tempt: 26 is the middle number (also the average). 24 is the smallest. 30 overshoots.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 24,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "If 3x − 2y = 7 and x + 2y = 5, then x + y =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "1"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "3"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "4"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "12"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "Add the equations: 4x = 12, so x = 3. Then 3 + 2y = 5 gives y = 1. So x + y = 4. Why the others tempt: 3 and 1 are x and y alone. 12 is 4x.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 25,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The solutions of x² − 3x = 10 are:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "x = 10 or x = 13"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "x = 2 or x = −5"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "x = 5 only"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "x = 5 or x = −2"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "Move everything to one side so the other side is zero: x² − 3x − 10 = 0, so (x − 5)(x + 2) = 0 and x = 5 or x = −2. Factors can only be set equal to a number when their product is zero. Why the others tempt: x = 10 or 13 comes from x(x − 3) = 10, setting each factor equal to 10. x = 2 or −5 flips the signs. “5 only” drops a root.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 26,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "The roots of 2x² − 4x + 5 = 0 are:",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "real and equal"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "non-real (complex)"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "real and distinct"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "rational and distinct"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "Discriminant = b² − 4ac = 16 − 40 = −24, which is negative, so there are no real roots. Why the others tempt: Real and equal needs a discriminant of 0. Real and distinct (or rational) needs a positive discriminant.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 27,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "How many positive integers satisfy 2x − 5 < 7?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "4"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "6"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "7"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "5"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "2x < 12, so x < 6 (strictly). The positive integers are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, which is 5 of them. Why the others tempt: 6 includes x = 6, which fails (7 is not less than 7). 7 also counts 0. 4 stops one short.",
      "sourceRef": {
        "page": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "n": 28,
      "type": "single",
      "question": "log₁₀ 4 + log₁₀ 25 =",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "1"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "100"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "log₁₀ 29 Geometry (Q29–Q34)"
        }
      ],
      "answer": [
        "B"
